@meta
id: 27-12
title: When vim does not know what it is
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: filetype
par: 27
minutes: 5
vscode: the language picker in the bottom-right corner
open: reconcile.py.20260917

@brief
This is the finance reconciliation script, restored from a dated backup
after last week's incident. Python, four-space indent, as Python always is.

The two lines of the body are flat against the margin and need one level
of indent. `>>` will give you two spaces, because two is what this repo's
TypeScript wants and vim has no reason to think otherwise: the file is
called `reconcile.py.20260917`, and there is no rule anywhere that says a
name ending in eight digits is Python.

Tell vim what it is looking at. The indent settings follow from that, and
so does everything else vim knows about Python.

@keys
| `:set ft?` | what vim currently thinks this buffer is |
| `:setf python` | set the filetype — the detection entry point |
| `:set filetype=python` | the same thing, and it re-runs even if already set |
| `:filetype detect` | make vim run its detection again from scratch |

@hints
Ask vim what filetype this buffer has before you change anything. The
answer is nothing at all, and every per-language setting follows from it.
--
`:setf python` and then indent as usual.
--
`:setf python` `Enter`, `2G`, `2>>`, `:wq` `Enter`.

@solution
    :setf python<Enter>  2G  2>>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-two keystrokes, and you never set `shiftwidth` at all.

That is the point. Setting the filetype fired vim's `FileType` event, which
loaded `ftplugin/python.vim`, which did this:

    setlocal shiftwidth=4 tabstop=4 expandtab softtabstop=4

along with the right `comments`, the right `formatoptions`, `%` jumping
between `if`/`elif`/`else`, and `[[`/`]]` moving by function. One command,
a dozen behaviours. This is why "vim indents my Python at four and my
TypeScript at two" is not something anyone configured — it is shipped, and
it happens the moment vim recognises the file.

`:setf` and `:set ft=` differ in one way worth knowing: `:setf` does
nothing if the filetype is already set, which is exactly what you want in
a detection rule, and `:set ft=` always applies, which is what you want
when you are overriding by hand.

If you hit this file often, the permanent fix is a detection rule rather
than a habit:

    au BufRead,BufNewFile *.py.* setf python

in `~/.vim/ftdetect/python.vim`. Same idea as chapter 27's other files:
vim already has a slot for it.

@start reconcile.py.20260917
def reconcile(payments, orders):
matched = [p for p in payments if p.order_id in orders]
return matched

@expected reconcile.py.20260917
def reconcile(payments, orders):
    matched = [p for p in payments if p.order_id in orders]
    return matched
